## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a multi‑tenant streaming pipeline with strict data isolation, governance, and production reliability on GCP. It probes concrete choices across Pub/Sub, Dataflow, BigQuery, Cloud SQL, and security controls.

## Key Concepts

- Pub/Sub streaming ingestion
- Dataflow windowed processing
- Per-tenant BigQuery datasets and catalog joins
- IAM Conditions and per‑tenant service accounts
- VPC Service Controls and Private Service Connect
- DLP redaction before load
- End‑to‑end testing and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation: redact emails/phone in a record
function redact(record){
  const r = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(record));
  r.payload = r.payload.replace(/[A-Za-z._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g,"REDACTED_EMAIL");
  r.payload = r.payload.replace(/\b\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}\b/g,"REDACTED_PHONE");
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle an on‑prem data source that misses a scheduled window?
- What metrics and alerts would you implement to detect cross‑tenant data leakage or schema drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world multi-tenant data pipelines demand strong isolation, auditable control planes, and robust failure handling. This question probes identity management, data residency, and operational resilience in GCP.

## Key Concepts
- Partner-signed URLs for secure ingestion
- Cloud Functions validation and Pub/Sub as a decoupler
- Dataflow (streaming/batch) with per-tenant partitioning
- CMEK and tenant-scoped IAM + Workload Identity Federation
- VPC Service Controls perimeter to prevent exfiltration
- Idempotent processing and replay protection
- Rollback and test strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: publish validated message to Pub/Sub from a Cloud Function (Node.js)
const {PubSub} = require('@google-cloud/pubsub');
const pubsub = new PubSub();
exports.validateAndPublish = async (event) => {
  const record = JSON.parse(event.body);
  // basic schema check
  if (!record.tenant_id || !record.file_uri) throw new Error('Invalid');
  await pubsub.topic('tenant-ingest').publish(Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(record)));
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate per-tenant data isolation in BigQuery? 
- How would you test handling of schema evolution and data drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills in designing a multi-tenant, event-driven API on GCP using API Gateway, Cloud Tasks, and Cloud Run, with per-tenant isolation and observable patterns. It focuses on real components, trade-offs, and testability.

## Key Concepts
- API Gateway
- Cloud Tasks
- Cloud Run
- per-tenant isolation (buckets and service accounts)
- idempotent processing
- observability (Cloud Logging/Monitoring)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dedupe check against Firestore
async function isDuplicate(eventId, tenantId) {
  const doc = await Firestore.collection('dedup').doc(`${tenantId}_${eventId}`).get();
  if (doc.exists) return true;
  await Firestore.collection('dedup').doc(`${tenantId}_${eventId}`).set({ seenAt: Date.now() });
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test idempotency and failure paths end-to-end?
- How would you monitor and alert on retries or quota exhaustion in Cloud Tasks?
